---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../../data/site';

const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Someone has died', url: '/someone-has-died/' }];
---

<Page
  title="Someone Has Died — What to Do Now"
  heading="Someone has died"
  description="What to do first when someone dies in East Lothian or Berwickshire: practical steps, who to call, and what happens next. Available day or night."
  standfirst="There is no hurry. Almost nothing has to be done in the next few hours, and we can help with all of it."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <Callout tone="accent" title="Call us whenever you are ready">
    <p>
      <a href={CONTACT.phoneHref} class="text-xl font-semibold">{CONTACT.phone}</a><br />
      One of us will answer, at any hour. There is no charge for the call and no
      obligation to use us afterwards.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    If someone has just died, the first thing worth saying is this: you have more time
    than you think. Unless you have been told otherwise, nothing needs to happen tonight.
    You do not have to decide anything about the funeral yet. You do not have to have
    chosen a funeral director to ring us and ask a question.
  </p>

  <h2>What has to happen first</h2>

  <p>
    Before anything else, a doctor needs to confirm the death and complete a Medical
    Certificate of Cause of Death. What that involves depends on where the person died.
  </p>

  <h3>At home</h3>
  <p>
    Telephone the GP practice during surgery hours, or NHS 24 on 111 out of hours. A
    doctor or nurse will come. If the death was expected, there is no emergency — you do
    not need to call 999. When you are ready, ring us and we will come to bring the
    person who has died into our care, at whatever hour suits you.
  </p>

  <h3>In hospital</h3>
  <p>
    The ward staff will guide you and will contact the hospital bereavement office. For
    the Royal Infirmary of Edinburgh and the other Lothian hospitals, that office will
    telephone you to arrange collection of the certificate. The person who has died will
    stay in the hospital mortuary until we collect them, so there is no rush at all.
  </p>

  <h3>In a care home</h3>
  <p>
    The home will contact the GP and will usually contact us on your behalf if you have
    told them who you would like to use. You are welcome to spend as long as you want
    with the person before they are moved.
  </p>

  <h3>Suddenly, or unexpectedly</h3>
  <p>
    If a death is sudden, unexplained or accidental, it is reported to the Procurator
    Fiscal. This is routine and does not mean anything is wrong. The Fiscal may ask for
    further examination before the death can be registered, which can add a few days.
    We deal with the Fiscal's office regularly and will keep you informed throughout.
  </p>

  <Callout title="What we do when you call">
    <p>
      We will ask a small number of practical questions — where the person is, whether a
      doctor has been, and whether there is anything we should know. Then we will arrange
      to bring them into our care. We do not discuss money on that first call unless you
      want to.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Then, in the following days</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Register the death.</strong> This must be done within eight days in
      Scotland. You can do it at any registration office in the country — it does not
      have to be where the person lived or died.{' '}
      <a href="/someone-has-died/registering-a-death/">How to register a death</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Meet us to arrange the funeral.</strong> At our funeral home, or at your
      own kitchen table if you would rather.{' '}
      <a href="/arranging-a-funeral/">What happens at that meeting</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tell the organisations that need to know.</strong> The registrar will offer
      you Tell Us Once, which notifies most government departments in a single step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check whether help with the cost is available.</strong> Funeral Support
      Payment can be paid straight to us, so the money never has to pass through your
      hands.{' '}
      <a href="/funeral-costs/help-with-funeral-costs/">Help with funeral costs</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Questions people ask us first</h2>

  <h3>Do I have to decide on a funeral director straight away?</h3>
  <p>
    No. And if you have already asked someone to bring the person into their care, you are
    still free to change your mind afterwards. You are never locked in.
  </p>

  <h3>Can I see the person who has died?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, whenever you would like, for as long as you would like. Some families come every
    day, some come once, some would rather not — all of that is normal. Just tell us and
    we will make the arrangements.
  </p>

  <h3>What does it cost to bring someone into your care?</h3>
  <p>
    Nothing, at that point. Our full prices are published{' '}
    <a href="/funeral-costs/standardised-price-list/">on our price list</a> and we will
    give you a written estimate before you commit to anything.
  </p>

  <h3>I am not in Dunbar. Can you still help?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. We look after families across East Lothian and Berwickshire, and we can bring
    someone home from anywhere in the country or from abroad.{' '}
    <a href="/funeral-services/repatriation/">More about repatriation</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>If you would rather read than telephone</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/someone-has-died/first-24-hours/">The first 24 hours, step by step</a></li>
    <li><a href="/someone-has-died/registering-a-death/">Registering a death in East Lothian</a></li>
    <li><a href="/arranging-a-funeral/">Arranging the funeral</a></li>
    <li><a href="/bereavement-support/">Bereavement support in the east of Scotland</a></li>
  </ul>
</Page>
